IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `tad-dates`
IMPORT `tad-rules`

-- Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) -- the questions of the PERSON AGGRIEVED (the complainant).
--
-- SEAT: the individual against whom discrimination or prohibited conduct was directed, who may complain to the
-- Anti-Discrimination Commissioner -- a worker or job applicant, a student, a tenant, a customer, a club member, a
-- person seeking insurance or superannuation, a person dealing with a State agency: "Was I discriminated against?",
-- "Was I sexually harassed?", "Was I victimised?", "Can they rely on an exception?", "Who else is liable?", "Can I
-- complain, and by when?", "By when must I ask for review, or appeal?", "What can the Tribunal order?", "Can I enforce
-- it?". The respondent, the Commissioner, the Tribunal and the Supreme Court get no seat: their judgments and decisions
-- are asked as facts. An exception is something the respondent must prove (s 101); it is asked here as "does it cover
-- what they did?", on facts the person may know or be told.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, numbers, dates, a choice from a list) and calls the rules of tad-rules.l4.
-- Three regulative rules -- the time limit for the complaint (s 63), the application for review of a rejection,
-- non-acceptance or dismissal (ss 65(3), (4), 71(4), (5)) and the appeal from a s 78(4) dismissal (s 100(2)) -- are
-- DEONTIC rules run inside the "on this day" questions; no question takes a DEONTIC or an EVENT.
--
-- UNOFFICIAL. Based on material from the Tasmanian Legislation website (State of Tasmania, CC BY 4.0); not the
-- authorised version. See SOURCE-LICENSE.md. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas): the questions of the person aggrieved`

§§ `What happened to me`

@export Is the reason they treated me badly a "prescribed attribute" under the Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) (s 16, s 3)? The Act protects race, age, sexual orientation, lawful sexual activity, gender, gender identity, sex characteristics, marital status, relationship status, pregnancy, breastfeeding, parental status, family responsibilities, disability, industrial activity, political belief or activity, religious belief or activity, an irrelevant criminal or medical record, and association with a person who has one of these. Nothing else counts.
GIVEN `the reason` IS AN `Attribute` @desc The attribute you were treated badly for, whether you have it, had it, or are believed to have it (or something else not listed)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is it a prescribed attribute` MEANS `s 16 -- it is a prescribed attribute` `the reason`

@export Was I directly discriminated against under the Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) (ss 14, 16, 22), before any exception they may prove? Direct discrimination is treating you less favourably than a person without the attribute, on the basis of an attribute you have or are believed to have, or a characteristic imputed to it; it need not be the only or main reason, and their motive does not matter. It must be in connection with employment, education and training, facilities, goods and services, accommodation, clubs, the administration of State laws or programs, or awards and industrial agreements.
GIVEN `the attribute` IS AN `Attribute` @desc The attribute you were treated badly for
      `the area` IS AN `Area` @desc Where it happened: employment (including unpaid work, partnerships, contractors, agencies and professional bodies); education and training; the provision of facilities, goods and services (including banking, insurance, transport, land dealings and State or council services); accommodation; membership and activities of clubs; the administration of a State law or program; awards, enterprise agreements or industrial agreements; or none of these
      `treated less favourably` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they treated you less favourably than they treat or would treat a person without the attribute, on the basis of the attribute or a characteristic imputed to it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Was I directly discriminated against` MEANS
    `s 14 -- direct discrimination` `the attribute` `the area` `treated less favourably`

@export Was I indirectly discriminated against under the Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) (ss 15, 16, 22), before any exception they may prove? Indirect discrimination is imposing a condition, requirement or practice that is unreasonable in the circumstances and disadvantages members of a group who share (or are believed to share) an attribute more than others; they need not know it does.
GIVEN `the attribute` IS AN `Attribute` @desc The attribute your group shares or is believed to share
      `the area` IS AN `Area` @desc Where it happened (as for direct discrimination), or none of these
      `they imposed a condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they imposed a condition, requirement or practice
      `is it unreasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the condition, requirement or practice is unreasonable in the circumstances
      `does it disadvantage my group more` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it disadvantages members of the group sharing the attribute more than people outside the group
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Was I indirectly discriminated against` MEANS
    `s 15 -- indirect discrimination` `the attribute` `the area` `they imposed a condition` `is it unreasonable` `does it disadvantage my group more`

@export Was the conduct that offended, humiliated, intimidated, insulted or ridiculed me prohibited by the Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) s 17(1)? It is, if it was on the basis of gender, race, age, sexual orientation, lawful sexual activity, gender identity, sex characteristics, disability, marital or relationship status, pregnancy, breastfeeding, parental status or family responsibilities, in circumstances where a reasonable person would have anticipated that effect, in one of the Act's areas. The public purpose exception (s 55) is asked separately. Where the answer is empty, s 17(1) does not reach the case on these facts.
GIVEN `the attribute` IS AN `Attribute` @desc The attribute the conduct was based on
      `the area` IS AN `Area` @desc Where it happened, or none of these
      `did it offend or humiliate me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conduct offended, humiliated, intimidated, insulted or ridiculed you on the basis of the attribute
      `would a reasonable person have anticipated it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a reasonable person, having regard to all the circumstances, would have anticipated that you would be offended, humiliated, intimidated, insulted or ridiculed
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Was the offensive conduct prohibited` MEANS
    `s 17(1) -- offensive conduct` `the attribute` `the area` `did it offend or humiliate me` `would a reasonable person have anticipated it`

@export Was I sexually harassed under the Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) s 17(2), (3)? It is unsolicited physical contact of a sexual nature; an unwelcome sexual advance or request for sexual favours; an unwelcome remark with sexual connotations to you or about you in your presence; an unwelcome gesture, action or comment of a sexual nature; or other conduct of a sexual nature offensive to you, where a reasonable person would have anticipated you would be offended, humiliated, intimidated, insulted or ridiculed, in one of the Act's areas.
GIVEN `what they did` IS A `Sexual conduct` @desc What they did, or none of these
      `the area` IS AN `Area` @desc Where it happened, or none of these
      `was it unwelcome` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was unsolicited (physical contact), unwelcome (an advance, request, remark, gesture, action or comment) or offensive to you (other conduct)
      `would a reasonable person have anticipated offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a reasonable person, having regard to all the circumstances, would have anticipated that you would be offended, humiliated, intimidated, insulted or ridiculed
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Was I sexually harassed` MEANS
    `s 17(2), (3) -- sexual harassment` `what they did` `the area` `was it unwelcome` `would a reasonable person have anticipated offence`

@export Was I victimised under the Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) s 18? Victimisation is subjecting you or your associate to any detriment (including humiliation or denigration), or threatening to, because you complained or intend to, gave or intend to give evidence or information in proceedings under the Act, alleged or intend to allege a contravention, refused or intend to refuse to contravene the Act, or did anything else under or by reference to the Act.
GIVEN `a detriment to me or my associate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they subjected you or an associate of yours to a detriment, or threatened to
      `why they did it` IS A `Reason for the detriment` @desc Why they did it
      `the area` IS AN `Area` @desc Where it happened, or none of these
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Was I victimised` MEANS
    `s 18 -- victimisation` `a detriment to me or my associate` `why they did it` `the area`

@export Was a public act inciting hatred towards, serious contempt for, or severe ridicule of me or my group prohibited by the Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) s 19? It is, if it was on the ground of race, disability, sexual orientation or lawful sexual activity, religious belief, affiliation or activity, gender identity or sex characteristics, in any area. The public purpose exception (s 55) is asked separately.
GIVEN `the ground` IS AN `Attribute` @desc The attribute of you or your group the act was aimed at
      `was it a public act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was a public act: a communication to the public, conduct observable by the public, or distribution or dissemination of matter to the public
      `did it incite hatred` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it incited hatred towards, serious contempt for, or severe ridicule of you or your group
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Was it inciting hatred` MEANS
    `s 19 -- inciting hatred` `the ground` `was it a public act` `did it incite hatred`

@export Was the sign, notice or advertisement prohibited by the Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) s 20? A person must not publish or display, or cause or permit to be published or displayed, a sign, notice or advertising matter that promotes, expresses or depicts discrimination or prohibited conduct, unless it is used to discourage discrimination or prohibited conduct.
GIVEN `did they publish or display it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person complained about published, displayed or authorised it, or caused or permitted it to be published or displayed
      `does it promote discrimination` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it promotes, expresses or depicts discrimination or prohibited conduct
      `is it used to discourage discrimination` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is used for the purpose of discouraging discrimination or prohibited conduct
      `the area` IS AN `Area` @desc The area it was connected with (for example a job advertisement is employment), or none of these
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Was the advertisement prohibited` MEANS
    `s 20 -- the sign or advertisement was prohibited` `did they publish or display it` `does it promote discrimination` `is it used to discourage discrimination` `the area`

§§ `Who is liable`

@export Is someone who knowingly caused, induced or helped the person who wronged me liable too (Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) s 21)? Everyone who knowingly causes, induces or aids another to contravene the Act is jointly and severally liable for the contravention.
GIVEN `did someone contravene the Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who wronged you contravened the Act
      `did another knowingly cause, induce or aid it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether another person knowingly caused, induced or aided them to do it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is the helper liable too` MEANS
    `s 21 -- the helper is jointly liable` `did someone contravene the Act` `did another knowingly cause, induce or aid it`

@export Is the organisation liable for what its member, officer, employee or agent did to me (Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) s 104)? An organisation is to ensure its people know about the Act and that none of them engages in discrimination or prohibited conduct; an organisation that does not comply is liable for any contravention its people commit. Read literally, a contravention by one of its people is itself a failure to comply.
GIVEN `is it an organisation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the body is an organisation (a company, association, council, Government department, State authority or other body)
      `was the person one of its people` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who wronged you was its member, officer, employee or agent
      `did that person contravene the Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that person contravened the Act
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is the organisation liable` MEANS
    `s 104 -- the organisation is liable` `is it an organisation` `was the person one of its people` `did that person contravene the Act`

§§ `Can they rely on an exception -- asked only if they say an exception or exemption covers what they did`

@export Does the exception they rely on cover what they did (Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) Part 5, ss 23-54, and an exemption granted under ss 56-57)? They must prove it on the balance of probabilities (s 101). Each exception covers only the grounds and the setting it names, and most also need a condition (shown after the colon in its name). An empty answer means the exception does not reach your case on its own words.
GIVEN `the exception` IS AN `Exception` @desc The exception or exemption they rely on
      `the attribute` IS AN `Attribute` @desc The attribute you were treated badly for
      `is it in the setting` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether what they did is in the setting the exception names: for example in employment, in a religious institution, in the provision of insurance or superannuation, a competitive sporting activity (not coaching, umpiring or administration), membership of a club, admission as a student, the conduct covered by a granted exemption that is in force; answer yes for ss 24, 25, 28 and 47, which name no setting
      `is its condition met` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the exception's condition is met: for example a genuine occupational qualification or requirement; reasonably necessary to comply with a law or order; unjustifiable hardship; required by religious doctrine; based on reliable actuarial or statistical data and reasonable; shared accommodation; a reasonable risk of disruption or danger if a child is not accompanied; for s 27(2), that the benefit cannot practicably be used by both genders at once. Any answer for exceptions with no condition
      `is its further condition met` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For s 27(2): whether the benefit is provided to men and women separately or in a fair and reasonable proportion; for ss 30, 33(1)(b), 34 and 44: whether they disclosed to the Tribunal, when required, the sources of the data and the factors relied on; for s 51A(1): whether you were not already enrolled as a student there. Any answer otherwise
      `the number` IS A NUMBER @desc For s 27(1)(e): how many adults share the accommodation; for ss 29 and 39: your age in years. Any number otherwise
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does the exception cover it` MEANS
    `Part 5 -- the exception covers it` `the exception` `the attribute` `is it in the setting` `is its condition met` `is its further condition met` `the number`

@export Does the public purpose exception cover the conduct (Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) s 55)? Offensive conduct under s 17(1) and inciting hatred under s 19 are not prohibited if the conduct is a fair report of a public act, a communication protected by absolute privilege in defamation, or a public act done in good faith for academic, artistic, scientific or research purposes or in the public interest.
GIVEN `the conduct` IS A `Conduct complained of` @desc The kind of conduct you complain of
      `was it a fair report` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was a fair report of a public act
      `was it absolutely privileged` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was a communication or dissemination of a matter subject to a defence of absolute privilege in defamation proceedings
      `was it in good faith for a public purpose` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was a public act done in good faith for academic, artistic, scientific or research purposes or any purpose in the public interest
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does the public purpose exception cover it` MEANS
    `s 55 -- the public purpose exception covers it` `the conduct` `was it a fair report` `was it absolutely privileged` `was it in good faith for a public purpose`

@export By when can I apply to the Tribunal to review the Commissioner's decision on an exemption (Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) s 59)? Within 28 days after the notice of a grant or renewal is published in the Gazette, after the applicant receives the statement of reasons for a refusal, or after a condition is imposed or varied; the application must be in writing.
GIVEN `the starting day` IS A DATE @desc The day the notice of grant or renewal was published in the Gazette, the day the statement of reasons for a refusal was received, or the day the condition was imposed or varied
GIVETH A DATE
`By when can I seek review of an exemption` MEANS
    `s 59 -- the last day to apply for review of an exemption` `the starting day`

§§ `Making a complaint`

@export May this complaint be made to the Anti-Discrimination Commissioner (Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) ss 60, 60A)? You may complain if it was directed against you; for a class you belong to, a union for a class of members, or an organisation it was directed against, only if the Commissioner is satisfied a majority are likely to consent; someone else on your behalf only with the Commissioner's approval; an agent, a union for one member, or a litigation guardian may complain. A child may complain only if the Commissioner is satisfied the child is mature enough.
GIVEN `who` IS A `Who is complaining` @desc Who is making the complaint
      `the age of the person complaining` IS A NUMBER @desc The age in years of the person making the complaint (for a union, organisation or agent, any number of 18 or more)
      `is the Commissioner satisfied a majority will consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a complaint on behalf of a class, or by an organisation: whether the Commissioner is satisfied that a majority of the members are likely to consent
      `did the Commissioner approve it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a complaint made on behalf of another person: whether the Commissioner approved it
      `is the Commissioner satisfied the child is mature enough` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a complainant under 18: whether the Commissioner is satisfied the child has sufficient maturity to make the complaint
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May the complaint be made` MEANS
    `s 60 -- the complaint may be made` `who` `the age of the person complaining` `is the Commissioner satisfied a majority will consent` `did the Commissioner approve it` `is the Commissioner satisfied the child is mature enough`

@export Does anything I did earlier, or the date, stop me complaining (Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) ss 66, 68, 73, 108)? The Act does not reach conduct that took place and ended before it commenced. After agreeing not to complain, or withdrawing a complaint about the same matter, you need the Commissioner's acceptance or permission; after a complaint about the same matter lapsed you cannot complain again.
GIVEN `did it all end before the Act commenced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conduct took place and ended before the Act commenced (the Act came into force on a day proclaimed under s 2; the day is not in the deposited text)
      `the earlier step` IS AN `Earlier step` @desc What happened earlier about the same matter, if anything
      `does the Commissioner allow it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner accepts the complaint as fair (after an agreement not to complain), gives permission (after a withdrawal), or allows it to be reinstituted in the public interest (after an involuntary withdrawal)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does anything stop me complaining` MEANS
    `ss 66, 68, 73, 108 -- nothing bars the complaint` `did it all end before the Act commenced` `the earlier step` `does the Commissioner allow it`

@export Is my complaint in the form the Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) s 62 requires? It must be in writing and signed by you, identify who the conduct was directed against and who the complaint is against, set out the details, and be lodged with the Commissioner in person, by post or by another means the Commissioner allows.
GIVEN `is it written and signed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the complaint is in writing and signed by you
      `does it identify the parties` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it identifies the person, class or organisation the conduct was directed against and the person it is made against
      `does it set out the details` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it sets out the details of the alleged discrimination or prohibited conduct
      `is it lodged properly` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is lodged with the Commissioner in person, by post or by another means the Commissioner allows
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my complaint in the required form` MEANS
    `s 62 -- the complaint is in the required form` `is it written and signed` `does it identify the parties` `does it set out the details` `is it lodged properly`

@export By when must I complain to the Commissioner (Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) s 63(1))? Within 12 months after the conduct took place: the last day is the same day of the month 12 months later.
GIVEN `the day it happened` IS A DATE @desc The day the discrimination or prohibited conduct took place
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I complain` MEANS `s 63 -- the last day to complain` `the day it happened`

@export If I complain on this day, is my complaint in time (Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) s 63)? A late complaint may still be accepted if the Commissioner is satisfied that it is reasonable to do so.
GIVEN `the day it happened` IS A DATE @desc The day the discrimination or prohibited conduct took place
      `the day I complain` IS A DATE @desc The day you make (or made) the complaint
GIVETH A `Complaint timing`
`Is my complaint in time` MEANS `s 63 -- the complaint is in time` `the day it happened` `the day I complain`

@export Can the Commissioner reject my complaint on this ground (Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) s 64(1))? Only on one of the grounds in s 64(1), several of which depend on the Commissioner's opinion or satisfaction.
GIVEN `the ground` IS A `Rejection ground` @desc The ground for rejecting the complaint, or none of these
      `is the ground made out` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the ground is made out (where the ground is the Commissioner's opinion or satisfaction, whether the Commissioner holds it)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my complaint be rejected` MEANS `s 64 -- the Commissioner may reject the complaint` `the ground` `is the ground made out`

@export By when must the Commissioner decide whether to accept or reject my complaint (Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) s 64(2))? Within 42 days after receiving it.
GIVEN `the day it was received` IS A DATE @desc The day the Commissioner received your complaint
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must my complaint be accepted or rejected` MEANS `s 64 -- the last day for the decision` `the day it was received`

§§ `If my complaint is rejected, not accepted or dismissed -- asked only if that has happened`

@export By when must I apply to the Tribunal to review the rejection, non-acceptance or dismissal of my complaint (Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) ss 65(3), 71(4))? Within 28 days after you receive the Commissioner's statement of reasons; the application must be in writing.
GIVEN `the day I received the statement` IS A DATE @desc The day you received the Commissioner's written statement of reasons (or notification of it)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I apply for review` MEANS `ss 65, 71 -- the last day to apply for review` `the day I received the statement`

@export If I apply for review on this day, is it in time, or has my complaint lapsed (Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) ss 65(3), (4), 71(4), (5), 73)? If you do not apply within 28 days the complaint lapses, and you cannot make another complaint about the same matter.
GIVEN `the day I received the statement` IS A DATE @desc The day you received the Commissioner's written statement of reasons (or notification of it)
      `the day I apply` IS A DATE @desc The day you apply (or applied) to the Tribunal
GIVETH A `Review timing`
`Is my review application in time` MEANS `ss 65, 71 -- the review application is in time` `the day I received the statement` `the day I apply`

@export What happens after the Tribunal reviews the Commissioner's decision on my complaint (Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) ss 72, 73)?
GIVEN `the decision` IS A `Commissioner decision` @desc What the Commissioner decided
      `was the decision correct` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal is satisfied the Commissioner's decision was correct
GIVETH A `Review outcome`
`What happens after review` MEANS `s 72 -- what the review leads to` `the decision` `was the decision correct`

§§ `Referral to the Tribunal and the inquiry`

@export By when must the Commissioner refer my complaint to the Tribunal for inquiry (Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) s 78(2))? Within 6 months after the respondent was notified that the complaint was accepted, or within any further period agreed with you, whether or not the investigation is complete.
GIVEN `the day the respondent was notified` IS A DATE @desc The day the respondent was notified of the acceptance of your complaint (s 67)
      `did I agree a further period` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you agreed a further period with the Commissioner
      `the agreed last day` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the agreed further period (any date if none)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must my complaint be referred` MEANS `s 78 -- the last day for referral` `the day the respondent was notified` `did I agree a further period` `the agreed last day`

@export By when can I appeal to the Supreme Court if the Commissioner dismisses my complaint for failing to pursue it (Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) ss 78(4), 100(2))? Within 28 days after the decision was made.
GIVEN `the day of the decision` IS A DATE @desc The day the Commissioner decided not to refer and to dismiss your complaint
GIVETH A DATE
`By when can I appeal a dismissal for not pursuing` MEANS `s 100 -- the last day to appeal` `the day of the decision`

@export If I appeal on this day, is my appeal against a dismissal for not pursuing the complaint in time (Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) s 100(2))?
GIVEN `the day of the decision` IS A DATE @desc The day the Commissioner decided not to refer and to dismiss your complaint
      `the day I appeal` IS A DATE @desc The day you appeal (or appealed) to the Supreme Court
GIVETH A `Appeal timing`
`Is my appeal in time` MEANS `s 100 -- the appeal is in time` `the day of the decision` `the day I appeal`

@export If I did not go to the Tribunal's directions conference, can the Tribunal dismiss my complaint and order me to pay the respondent's costs (Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) s 80(4))? Only if you were required to attend and did not, without reasonable excuse.
GIVEN `was I required to attend` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal required you, by written notice, to attend the directions conference
      `did I attend` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you attended
      `did I have a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you had a reasonable excuse for not attending
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my complaint be dismissed for not attending` MEANS `s 80 -- the Tribunal may dismiss and order costs` `was I required to attend` `did I attend` `did I have a reasonable excuse`

§§ `What the Tribunal can order`

@export Can the Tribunal make this order on my complaint (Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) ss 89, 91, 92)? Only if it finds after an inquiry that the complaint is substantiated. Then it may order the respondent not to repeat the conduct, to redress your loss, injury or humiliation, to re-employ you, to pay you compensation, to pay a fine of up to 20 penalty units, to vary or void a contract, to apologise or retract, or any other appropriate order; for a State Service respondent, it may order the Minister to act; for an award or agreement, it must refer it to the Industrial Commission.
GIVEN `the order` IS AN `Order` @desc The order you are asking about
      `was it substantiated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal found after an inquiry that your complaint is substantiated
      `is the respondent a State Service officer or employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the respondent is a State Service officer or State Service employee
      `does it relate to an award or agreement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the complaint relates to an award, enterprise agreement or industrial agreement
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Tribunal make this order` MEANS `ss 89, 91, 92 -- the Tribunal may make the order` `the order` `was it substantiated` `is the respondent a State Service officer or employee` `does it relate to an award or agreement`

@export What is the largest fine the Tribunal can order the respondent to pay on my substantiated complaint, in penalty units (Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) s 89(1)(e))? 20 penalty units; the dollar value of a penalty unit is set under other Tasmanian law.
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the largest fine on the respondent` MEANS `s 89 -- the largest fine on the respondent in penalty units`

@export What is the largest fine I can be ordered to pay if the Tribunal dismisses my complaint, in penalty units (Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) s 99(3))? Up to 10 penalty units if it is dismissed as vexatious; s 99(3) gives no fine otherwise.
GIVEN `was it dismissed as vexatious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal dismissed your complaint on the ground that it is vexatious
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the largest fine on me` MEANS `s 99 -- the largest fine on the complainant in penalty units` `was it dismissed as vexatious`

@export By when must I ask the Tribunal for written reasons for its order (Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) s 93)? Within 28 days after the date of the determination, in writing.
GIVEN `the day of the determination` IS A DATE @desc The date of the Tribunal's determination
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I ask for reasons` MEANS `s 93 -- the last day to ask for reasons` `the day of the determination`

@export Can I enforce the Tribunal's order or the agreement resolving my complaint as an order of the Supreme Court (Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) ss 76(4), 90, 94(4))? Yes, once you file, free of charge in the Supreme Court, a certified copy of the order or the record of the agreement and an affidavit saying how far it has not been complied with.
GIVEN `what` IS A `What to enforce` @desc What you want to enforce
      `have I filed the documents` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you (or the Commissioner at your request) have filed the certified copy and the affidavit in the Supreme Court
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I enforce it in the Supreme Court` MEANS `s 90 -- enforceable as a Supreme Court order` `what` `have I filed the documents`

§§ `Protection for complaining`

@export Am I protected from being sued for complaining or giving evidence (Anti-Discrimination Act 1998 (Tas) s 102)? You are not liable for another person's loss, damage or injury only because you, in good faith, lodged a complaint with the Commissioner or gave information or evidence to the Commissioner or Tribunal.
GIVEN `did I complain or give evidence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you lodged a complaint with the Commissioner or gave information or evidence to the Commissioner or Tribunal
      `was it in good faith` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you did so in good faith
      `is the claim only because of that` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the claim against you is made only because you did so
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I protected from being sued` MEANS `s 102 -- I am immune` `did I complain or give evidence` `was it in good faith` `is the claim only because of that`
